r  bedroom doesn't have just one mean that  noise is added to it has a different  mean for each city and state now we're  still - you are still saying here that  we're giving it the list of cities that  exist in the list in the list of states  that exist and this might seem like a  more reasonable assumption we often do  know what the possible values for some  column are but it's kind of annoying to  compile and sometimes we don't know it  so P clean is also going to let you  replace that with an underscore and that  means that it should learn which  of the things in that column are  actually valid values and which ones  were just erroneous so for example if we  have a big data set and only one row has  the city Newport in it the system is  going to compare the hypotheses that on  the one hand it's possible that there is  a city called Newport because we haven't  told it what cities exist so it's  possible that there is a city called  Newport but if that were the case it  would have such a small population that  only one entry from this table came from  it and so then it's comparing the  probability that we got the one citizen  of Newport versus the possibility that  one of the millions of citizens from New  York made a typo right and so it's going  to correct it to New York on the other  hand if we have several rows called  Newport and the rents are very low now  the calculus kind of changes because on  the one hand it can consider the  possibility that all of these people  made this typo same typo and that they  somehow all happen to be the ones who  got the cheapest rents in New York or it  can consider that there actually is a  small city called Newport in New York  that has cheap rents right and in this  case it will prefer the second  hypothesis and none of this came from us  explicitly coding these heuristics in  pea clean is based on probability theory  which is the lingua franca of a lot of  research in data science so I want to  show you one School benefit that we get  kind of for free by using probability  theory here which is that we can benefit  from some of the latest advances in AI  to create more expressive models so I  don't know if you've seen it was in the  news a while back that open a I had it  rained this huge language model called  GPT to GPT - they said it was so good at  generating language that it would be  dangerous for them to release it to the  world so they've been going on this like  small release strategy where they  released slowly more accurate models so  this is based on one of the medium  models that they've released what I'm  going to show you and the idea is that  you can prompt this this neural network  with a string it can be a pretty long  string so here I've put the abstract of  this talk ending within this talk I'll  introduce okay and it's going to  complete that string for us using what  it's learned from billions of documents  over the web so it chooses to say in  this talk I'll introduce X table which  enables Commons  reasoning to code up automated fixes and  checklists who automate common errors  and get your data clean faster don't  worry it includes a free demo I don't  appreciate it making promises on my  behalf but the idea is that that since  this is actually just all based on  probability theory all of these numbers  that we need in order to add one of  these primitive distributions are  available to us from GPT too so we can  add a new distribution called GPT to  elaborate a new primitive that we can  use in our models that's going to take  in a prompt and return and a completion  an elaboration of that of that string  according to GPT - this allows us to  model new date new kinds of datasets so  here's a data set of reviews it has  three columns whether a review is  positive what the product type being  reviewed is and the actual text of  review and these are actual Amazon  reviews and you can see some of the  cells are missing so what we can do is  we can write a pea clean script that  says okay here's how the data is  generated first we flip a coin I don't  know what the weight is to determine  whether we're gonna write a positive or  negative review and I don't know the  weight because maybe people are more  positive or more negative in general as  a whole let's not assume anything by  writing point five or something like  that then we're going to choose a  product type and you will just learn  what product types exist by looking at  the table itself and we'll learn what  proportion they occur in and then we  somehow want to say write or review that  that's based on is positive and the  product type and the idea is that we're  going to use GPT to elaborate on some  prompt to do that we're going to prompt  GPT - we could use the prompt the  following is my review and then just  have GPT to write a review but then GPT  twos review will not be based on the  sentiment and the product type that we  just sampled right there's no  relationship between these two so we  could instead say the following is my  review of this book or of this movie or  of this app and that's going to make it  more likely to generate reviews that are  about the product but we want to  incorporate the sentiment too so we can  switch on the sentiment with an if  statement and say if the if we're trying  to generate a positive review we're  going to prompt it by saying I'm giving  this book a very positive review because  it's fantastic a plus five out of five  best book ever like and otherwise we're  gonna say I'm giving this book a very  bad review because it's awful  one out of five truly terrible worst  book ever like and then we're gonna pass  that to GPT - to finish off the review  okay once we've done that once we've  included our assumptions about how the  review text depends on the other columns  right P clean can figure out that this  was a positive review about a book or a  negative review about a movie and we  didn't have to do any additional  training we didn't have to train a  neural net with a bunch of labeled  examples all of all we had to do was  specify the task by writing up this  prompt and and writing up this script so  I said there were two challenges we've  looked at the first challenge the second  one I'm going to spend less time on  because I've got just I want to spend  another five minutes or so this this  task look that I'm calling cleaning in  this example more generally is called  probabilistic inference it's about how  you infer the values of unknown  variables given domain knowledge and  your known variables and it's one of the  things that probabilistic programming  languages give us a lot of tools to do  so the architecture of PE and how it  actually works is that we take this  script that the user has written and we  compile it into a probabilistic model in  a general-purpose probabilistic  programming language called gen and  we're also going to extract some  metadata from this script that gen  wouldn't know about because they're sort  of things that are specific to two data  cleaning so we're going to extract from  the script sort of what fields are clean  what fields have the possibility of  being dirty which fields have parameters  which were these underscores what our  initial parameter values are we're going  to infer sort of there's also stuff that  I haven't shown about missingness we're  going to get metadata about sort of  aspects of the parameter estimation  process because none of that is sort of  built into gen but what we get by having  this probabilistic model represented in  gen is that it gives us the ability to  evaluate probabilities to express the  operation of conditioning our simulator  unknown observed values and most  importantly it gives us a library of  building blocks for approximate  inference and this is something that  couldn't have been done until very  recently because for a long time  probably stick programming languages  were just push button just like peek  Lina's you specify your model you say  clean and you hope that the algorithm  say infer and you hope  the algorithm can handle your model  right in djenne instead it gives us a  library of building blocks where we can  construct our own inference algorithm  and the inference algorithm behind peak  lien is making use of that we're using  several of the building blocks together  to create an inference program that  combines sequential Monte Carlo and  expectation-maximization by using these  building blocks it's something that Jenn  calls programmable inference and there  was another talk at strange Luke  yesterday by my colleague Marco about  that so feel free to go online and look  it explains sort of the new programming  constructs that enable the expression of  these kinds of inference algorithms the  nice thing about peak lean and in  general I hope about sort of these  domain-specific probabilistic  programming languages is that once  you've restricted yourself to a certain  domain you can reason much more  effectively about what sorts of  inference algorithms will always work in  that domain and so the idea is that this  inference algorithm though I'm sure that  we still need to work on it's um  it should be applicable to most of the  models that people write in Pete clean  because Pete clean isn't quite as  expressive as Jenn as a whole right we  have some preliminary results very  preliminary results sort of showing that  these existing machine learning based  systems for data cleaning we can match  them if we just encode some basic domain  knowledge and into our scripts but I  want to zoom out a little bit at the  very end because my point today isn't  primarily about pee clean or data  cleaning  it's about this broader idea of  probabilistic scripting the basic point  is that if you have some code that's  imperative and relies on a bunch of  heuristics right it's often because  you're trying to do something common  sense that the computer doesn't have t